<toolkit:Popup x:TypeArguments="services:PaymentSheetResult"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:toolkit="clr-namespace:CommunityToolkit.Maui.Views;assembly=CommunityToolkit.Maui"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:CafePosApp.Views"
             xmlns:services="clr-namespace:CafePos.Presentation.Services;assembly=CafePos.Presentation"
             x:Class="CafePosApp.Views.PaymentSheetPopup"
             x:DataType="views:PaymentSheetPopup"
             x:Name="Sheet"
             VerticalOptions="End"
             HorizontalOptions="Fill"
             Margin="0"
             BackgroundColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Surface}, Dark={StaticResource SurfaceDark}}">
    <!-- VerticalOptions="Start" on the ScrollView is what makes this a sheet rather than a panel —
         the same measured fix as CatalogActionSheetPopup (a ScrollView fills whatever height it is
         offered, so without Start the sheet came out 823dp tall on a 914dp screen for 292dp of
         content). The dim and outside-tap dismissal come from PopupOptions in Services.PaymentSheet,
         not from here, because the toolkit insets popup content by 15dp per side. -->
    <ScrollView x:Name="SheetScroll" VerticalOptions="Start">
        <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="SheetContent" Padding="16,8,16,16" Spacing="0">
            <BoxView Style="{StaticResource BottomSheetHandle}" />

            <!-- The caller's title: "Оплата заказа" at checkout, the order title on a top-up. -->
            <Label x:Name="TitleLabel" FontSize="15" Margin="0,12,0,4"
                   TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray600}, Dark={StaticResource Gray400}}"
                   LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />

            <!-- What is still owed — or, on a refund, the most that can be given back. The caption is
                 written from code because the direction changes what the number MEANS: the same
                 220.00 ₽ is a debt in one mode and a ceiling in the other, and a caption reading
                 "К оплате" over money going out would be actively wrong. -->
            <Label x:Name="AmountDueLabel" FontSize="18" FontAttributes="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,2" />

            <!-- Only on a top-up or a partial refund: how much has already been settled in the same
                 direction, so the sheet reads as a part-settlement rather than as a second full
                 charge or a second full return. -->
            <Label x:Name="AlreadyPaidLabel" FontSize="12" Margin="0,0,0,6"
                   TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray600}, Dark={StaticResource Gray400}}" />

            <!-- Refund only. Names where the money goes, because the operator has to hand cash to a
                 customer or credit a terminal, and the sheet is the last place before they do. -->
            <Label x:Name="RefundNoteLabel" FontSize="12" Margin="0,0,0,6" IsVisible="False" LineBreakMode="WordWrap"
                   TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray600}, Dark={StaticResource Gray400}}" />

            <!-- ═══ ONE TAP: THE COMMON SALE ═══
                 Two full-width tender buttons, each naming the amount it charges and the method it
                 charges it by — «160,00 ₽ наличными» / «160,00 ₽ картой». Tapping one records that
                 payment for exactly AmountDue and closes the sheet. No keypad in the way.

                 THE AMOUNT IS ON THE BUTTON ON PURPOSE. Baymard's 2024 study captured a tester
                 asking, of a «Next» button, verbatim: "I'm not sure if I click the 'Next' button,
                 will it charge?" A button naming only a METHOD repeats that ambiguity, because the
                 amount it would charge is somewhere else on the screen and has to be re-read. A
                 button naming the amount it charges answers the question from its own label, so the
                 button is complete on its own and the two are equal in what they state.

                 BOTH ARE FILLED PRIMARY, and neither is the "primary" one. Cash and card each
                 complete the sale and the sheet has no acquirer behind the card path at all
                 (PaymentSheetResult.Method records only what the operator says happened), so making
                 one of them quieter would be an opinion the app does not have. They are a genuine
                 fork, and both sides of it look like the thing you do — which is the opposite of
                 what the deferred button below must not be.

                 HIDDEN on a refund: quick pay is COLLECT-only. A cash/card choice on a return is
                 meaningless (see MethodGrid) and the operator must choose how much to give back,
                 so a one-tap «return everything the ceiling allows» would be the most dangerous
                 button in the app. The refund path keeps the keypad it always had.

                 ITS TEXT IS WRITTEN FROM CODE, not bound, for the same reason AmountDueLabel is:
                 PaymentSheetRequest is not this popup's binding context (see ApplyMode), and the
                 figure has to come from TextFormat.Money so it reads in the operator's currency.

                 «Другая сумма» lives INSIDE this panel, so hiding quick pay hides the way back into
                 the keypad with it. That is deliberate: a «Другая сумма» link left on screen while
                 the keypad is up would be a second button offering to open the keypad again, one
                 tap from the keys it opened.

                 8dp ABOVE the first tender button, and it is a measured gap, not a guess: with none
                 the sheet put the «160,00 ₽ наличными» button ~4dp under «К оплате: 160,00 ₽»,
                 tighter than the 8dp between the two tender buttons — so the amount and the
                 buttons read as one crowded block instead of a figure and the action on it. The
                 figure is the thing the operator is reading; the buttons are the thing they do. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="QuickPayPanel" Spacing="0" Margin="0,8,0,0">
                <Button x:Name="QuickPayCashButton" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                        FontSize="16" FontAttributes="Bold" CornerRadius="8" Padding="0"
                        Clicked="OnTenderClicked" CommandParameter="cash" />
                <Button x:Name="QuickPayCardButton" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                        Margin="0,8,0,0" FontSize="16" FontAttributes="Bold" CornerRadius="8" Padding="0"
                        Clicked="OnTenderClicked" CommandParameter="card" />

                <!-- The partial-payment exit, and it is BELOW the tender row on purpose. Partial
                     payment is real and supported (Order.BalanceKopecks and
                     PaymentState.PartiallyPaid exist for it: a deposit against an order), so it
                     stays; but it is not the common sale and it must not look like it.
                     SecondaryButton rather than a filled Primary, and 48dp tall like everything
                     else — a 30dp text link would be below the touch floor (.opencode-rules.md §6
                     calls 48dp the floor and notes it is already under the ergonomic minimum) and
                     would be the one control here an operator aiming at the card button misses. -->
                <Button x:Name="OtherAmountButton" Text="Другая сумма" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}"
                        HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48" Margin="0,10,0,0"
                        Clicked="OnOtherAmountClicked"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="Ввести другую сумму оплаты" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>

            <!-- NOTHING OWED. No tender buttons at all — there is nothing to charge, so the sheet
                 must not offer to charge it, and a keypad over a zero balance would be asking the
                 operator to decide something that cannot be decided (the same defect as offering a
                 keypad on the card path). The label is here so the sheet is not a title and a
                 figure with no way forward: an operator who opens a payment sheet with 0 ₽ owed
                 deserves to be told the reason, not left to work it out from missing buttons.

                 The «Оплата при выдаче» exit below is NOT hidden along with the tender buttons: it
                 takes no money, so it is not one, and on a nil cart total it is the only way to
                 complete the checkout. -->
            <Label x:Name="NothingDueLabel" IsVisible="False" FontSize="14" Margin="0,8,0,0"
                   LineBreakMode="WordWrap"
                   TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray600}, Dark={StaticResource Gray400}}" />

            <!-- ═══ THE KEYPAD: OFF THE MAIN PATH, NOT GONE ═══
                 EnteredLabel, ChangeLabel, the twelve keys, the method row and the confirm button,
                 together in one panel that is hidden while the tender buttons are up. It is a panel
                 rather than a set of per-element visibilities so the two states cannot disagree:
                 RefreshPanels owns the swap and there is exactly one thing to be wrong.

                 On a REFUND this panel is what the sheet is, unchanged — the refund path has no
                 quick pay and never had a «Другая сумма» link. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="KeypadPanel" Spacing="0">
                <!-- The amount being entered. The largest text on the keypad panel: it is what the
                     operator reads back to the customer before confirming. -->
                <Label x:Name="EnteredLabel" FontSize="32" FontAttributes="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,2"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center" SemanticProperties.Description="Введённая сумма" />

                <!-- Shown only when the entered amount exceeds the balance. The service clamps the
                     payment to the balance, but the change the operator must hand back is
                     entered − balance, so that is what is displayed.

                     Static value, so an AppThemeBinding on the attribute is the whole fix and needs no
                     ViewModel change. A bare {StaticResource Warning} declared the light token in both
                     themes, on this popup's own Surface fill (#FFFFFF / #1E1E1E): 3.11:1 in light and
                     5.36:1 in dark. The dark branch now reads WarningDark #FFB74D at 9.63:1.

                     THE LIGHT BRANCH IS NO LONGER BLOCKED, because Colors.xaml now carries
                     WarningText #A35B00 — the light-theme text counterpart of WarningDark, which is the
                     pair this attribute has wanted since the dark-theme pass. Warning could not be
                     darkened from here (it is the palette's only warm token and 3.11:1 on pure white is
                     the ceiling it can reach on ANY light background), so the fix belongs to the
                     palette, not to this file; it does, and this label uses it:
                       WarningText #A35B00 on Surface #FFFFFF   5.18:1  (light, was 3.11:1)
                       WarningDark #FFB74D on SurfaceDark #1E1E1E 9.63:1 (dark, unchanged)
                     14pt bold owes 4.5:1 as body text; 5.18:1 clears it. The hue is the same warning
                     family (33.5deg between Warning's 27.1 and WarningDark's 35.7), so «Сдача» still
                     reads as amber — and «Сдача» is the word that states the state, the tint is the
                     second cue. Nothing about the message changed. -->
                <Label x:Name="ChangeLabel" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,6"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center" LineBreakMode="WordWrap"
                       TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource WarningText}, Dark={StaticResource WarningDark}}" />

                <!-- The keypad is buttons, not an Entry: the OS keyboard covers half the screen and is
                     slow, and an Entry would bring it up on every tap. Every key is 48dp tall, the
                     floor the whole app honours. The whole-rubles cap is in OnKeyClicked and its
                     comment there explains it. -->
                <Grid x:Name="KeypadGrid" ColumnDefinitions="*,*,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto"
                      ColumnSpacing="8" RowSpacing="8" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                    <Button Text="1" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="1" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 1" />
                    <Button Text="2" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="2" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 2" />
                    <Button Text="3" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="3" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 3" />
                    <Button Text="4" Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="4" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 4" />
                    <Button Text="5" Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="5" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 5" />
                    <Button Text="6" Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="6" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 6" />
                    <Button Text="7" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="7" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 7" />
                    <Button Text="8" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="8" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 8" />
                    <Button Text="9" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="9" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 9" />
                    <Button Text="Сброс" Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="clear" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Очистить введённую сумму" />
                    <Button Text="0" Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="0" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Цифра 0" />
                    <Button Text="Стереть" Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource KeypadButton}" CommandParameter="backspace" Clicked="OnKeyClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Стереть последнюю цифру" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- Cash or card, single select. The selected one is filled Primary so the choice is
                     visible at a glance; the unselected one is the tonal secondary. It exists only on
                     the keyed path: the two tender buttons above settle a card payment without ever
                     asking, because a card is ALWAYS the balance — there is nothing to choose, and a
                     keypad there would be asking the operator to decide something that cannot be
                     decided. Here the operator has chosen «Другая сумма», so the method is a real
                     question and the row reappears. -->
                <!-- The whole grid is hidden on a refund, not merely ignored: a refund has no method, because
                     the domain mirrors the payments it reverses so the drawer and the terminal each
                     balance against what they were actually credited. A visible-but-ignored choice
                     would be worse than none — the operator would reasonably believe the return was
                     booked the way they picked. -->
                <Grid x:Name="MethodGrid" ColumnDefinitions="*,*" ColumnSpacing="8" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                    <Button x:Name="CashButton" Grid.Column="0" Text="Наличные" Style="{StaticResource PaymentMethodButton}"
                            CommandParameter="cash" Clicked="OnMethodClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Оплата наличными" />
                    <Button x:Name="CardButton" Grid.Column="1" Text="Карта" Style="{StaticResource PaymentMethodButton}"
                            CommandParameter="card" Clicked="OnMethodClicked" SemanticProperties.Description="Оплата картой" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- It USED TO BE DISABLED at an entered amount of 0, and that is what changed. A greyed
                     button states that the action is unavailable and says nothing about why; «оплатить
                     ноль» is refused because a zero-value row in OrderPayment would still be written,
                     would not move PaidKopecks, and would leave the shift reconciliation off by that
                     row. None of that is an operator's problem to infer, so the button stays live and
                     the refusal below NAMES it.

                     It is also why the refusal sits UNDER the confirm button rather than above it: the
                     sheet grows by a couple of lines when the message appears, and the control the
                     operator's thumb is already on must not move out from under it. -->
                <Button x:Name="ConfirmButton" Text="Принять оплату" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                        FontAttributes="Bold" Padding="0" Clicked="OnConfirmClicked"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="Принять оплату и продолжить" />

                <!-- Shown only after a confirm attempt at 0. WarningText/WarningDark for the same
                     measured reason ChangeLabel uses them (5.18:1 light / 9.63:1 dark on this popup's
                     Surface), and the amber is the second cue, not the only one: the sentence below
                     states the state in words, so nothing here is carried by colour. -->
                <Label x:Name="ZeroRefusalLabel" IsVisible="False" FontSize="13" FontAttributes="Bold"
                       Margin="0,8,0,0" LineBreakMode="WordWrap"
                       TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource WarningText}, Dark={StaticResource WarningDark}}" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>

            <!-- ═══ «ОПЛАТА ПРИ ВЫДАЧЕ» — THE OTHER ANSWER TO THE SAME QUESTION ═══
                 This button used to sit on the cart, beside «Оплатить и создать». It is here now
                 because it is not a second kind of checkout — it is a decision about WHEN money moves,
                 so it belongs at the moment money moves, beside the button that takes it.

                 ONE ROW, ONE SEPARATOR, and it is the LAST thing in the sheet so the primary action is
                 the one the thumb lands on first. SecondaryButton rather than a filled Primary: the
                 two filled buttons above it DO look like the thing you do, because taking the money
                 is the thing you do, and this one must not share that look. One tap did not change
                 the argument, it sharpened it — with «160,00 ₽ наличными» sitting above, a filled
                 «Оплата при выдаче» would be claiming to charge when it charges nothing, which is
                 the one claim on this sheet that would be outright false.

                 VISIBLE ONLY WHEN THE CALLER ASKED FOR IT, and that is load-bearing rather than
                 cosmetic. This sheet is also the payment step for an order that already exists: the
                 board's top-up and «Принять оплату» on the details page both open it, and on those
                 paths the order is already on the board with a balance outstanding. Offering to
                 CREATE one there, or to defer a payment for a sale already made, would put a
                 create-order action on the collect-payment path. Set from code, never bound —
                 PaymentSheetRequest is not the binding context here — see ApplyMode.

                 It is NOT disabled at 0 entered, and never was: the amount is irrelevant on this
                 path precisely because no money is being taken, so a disabled confirm beside it
                 would be the only control on the sheet that knows something the operator cannot
                 see. It stays live in BOTH panel states — deferring is not a fallback for being
                 unable to pay the balance, it is the other answer to the same question. -->
            <!-- A 1px rule, the same idiom MenuPage.xaml uses between the cart and its footer. NOT the
                 BottomSheetHandle style: that one is a 40dp centred grab bar, and reusing it here would
                 draw a second handle halfway down the sheet. -->
            <BoxView x:Name="DeferredSeparator" IsVisible="False" HeightRequest="1" Margin="0,12,0,10"
                     Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray200}, Dark={StaticResource Gray600}}" />
            <Button x:Name="DeferredButton" IsVisible="False" Text="Оплата при выдаче"
                    Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                    FontAttributes="Bold" Clicked="OnDeferredClicked"
                    SemanticProperties.Description="Создать заказ и записать оплату, когда клиент заберёт его" />
        </VerticalStackLayout>
    </ScrollView>
</toolkit:Popup>
